\section{Method}
\label{sec:method}

We compare three formulations for execution-free code repair verification, differing in output granularity and generation requirements. Given a candidate patch and its associated test suite, each formulation predicts correctness at a different level of abstraction. We then analyze the failure modes specific to generation-based verification and develop an oracle framework that characterizes when per-test granularity provides value.

\subsection{Problem Setup}
\label{sec:problem-setup}

Let $p$ denote a candidate code patch and $T = \{t_1, \ldots, t_n\}$ its associated test suite. Each test $t_i$ has a ground-truth label $y_i \in \{\textsc{Pass}, \textsc{Fail}\}$ determined by actual execution. The test suite decomposes into two disjoint subsets: \emph{fail-to-pass} tests $T_{\text{f2p}} \subseteq T$, which fail before the patch and should pass after a correct fix, and \emph{pass-to-pass} tests $T_{\text{p2p}} \subseteq T$, which pass before and should continue passing. A patch is correct if and only if all tests pass: $\forall\, t_i \in T{:}\ y_i = \textsc{Pass}$.

The verification task is to predict patch correctness without executing the code. We study three formulations that differ in prediction granularity and output format.

\subsection{Per-Test Binary Classification (\textsc{CLS})}
\label{sec:cls}

The classification formulation defines a function $f$ that predicts a binary verdict for each test independently:
%
\begin{equation}
  f(c_p, t_i) \rightarrow \hat{y}_i \in \{\textsc{Pass}, \textsc{Fail}\}
  \label{eq:cls}
\end{equation}
%
where $c_p$ is the code context containing the patched function and $t_i$ is the test input (function call with expected output). The model produces a \emph{single output token}, and the patch-level prediction is obtained by conjunction: $\hat{y}_p = \bigwedge_{i=1}^{n} [\hat{y}_i = \textsc{Pass}]$.

Training uses standard cross-entropy loss on the binary labels. At inference, each test requires exactly one generated token, making per-test cost constant regardless of the complexity of the underlying execution.

\subsection{Execution Output Generation (\textsc{CWM})}
\label{sec:cwm}

The generation formulation, following Code World Models \citep{ding2024cwm}, defines a function $g$ that predicts the execution output as a free-form string:
%
\begin{equation}
  g(c_p, t_i) \rightarrow \hat{o}_i \in \Sigma^*
  \label{eq:cwm}
\end{equation}
%
where $\hat{o}_i$ is the predicted output string and $\Sigma^*$ is the set of all finite strings over the token vocabulary. A binary verdict is then derived by exact match against the expected output $o_i^*$:
%
\begin{equation}
  \hat{y}_i = \begin{cases} \textsc{Pass} & \text{if } \hat{o}_i = o_i^* \\ \textsc{Fail} & \text{otherwise} \end{cases}
  \label{eq:cwm-verdict}
\end{equation}

Training uses standard language modeling loss on the output strings. At inference, each test requires generating $|\hat{o}_i|$ tokens, where the output length varies with the complexity of the executed computation. The total generation cost for a patch is $\sum_{i=1}^{n} |\hat{o}_i|$ tokens.

\subsection{Trajectory-Level Scoring (\textsc{TRAJ})}
\label{sec:traj}

The trajectory-level formulation defines a function $h$ that predicts a single binary verdict for the entire patch:
%
\begin{equation}
  h(c_p, T) \rightarrow \hat{y}_p \in \{\textsc{Resolved}, \textsc{Unresolved}\}
  \label{eq:traj}
\end{equation}
%
where the input includes the full test suite description rather than individual tests. This formulation produces no per-test signal---it cannot identify which specific tests a patch fails. We include \textsc{TRAJ} as a control to isolate the effect of prediction granularity: any accuracy difference between \textsc{CLS} and \textsc{TRAJ} at the patch level reflects the value (or cost) of per-test decomposition, independent of the classification-vs-generation distinction.

\subsection{CWM Failure Modes}
\label{sec:failure-modes}

The generation formulation introduces two systematic failure modes absent in classification:

\paragraph{Token-budget exhaustion.} When the expected output $o_i^*$ exceeds the model's generation budget $B$ (i.e., $|o_i^*| > B$), the predicted output $\hat{o}_i$ is necessarily truncated, guaranteeing exact-match failure regardless of prediction quality. This affects tests involving long outputs---complex data structures, multi-line traces, or iterative computations---which become systematically unverifiable under \textsc{CWM}.

\paragraph{String-value prediction errors.} Even within the token budget, exact match requires character-level fidelity. Semantically equivalent outputs that differ in surface form---floating-point precision, whitespace conventions, dictionary ordering---produce false negatives. The probability of such errors grows with output complexity and the number of independently predicted tokens: each additional token introduces a multiplicative chance of mismatch.

\textsc{CLS} bypasses both failure modes by construction: its single-token output cannot be truncated and requires no string matching. We hypothesize that this asymmetry produces a \emph{complexity-dependent advantage}: on simple code with short execution outputs, both formulations perform comparably because CWM failure modes rarely trigger; on complex code with long and structured outputs, the advantage of \textsc{CLS} grows as failure modes increasingly affect \textsc{CWM}.

\subsection{Oracle Accuracy-Utility Framework}
\label{sec:oracle}

Per-test verification provides finer-grained information than trajectory-level scoring, but this granularity is only valuable when (a) per-test predictions are sufficiently accurate and (b) multiple tests per patch provide partially independent signals. We formalize these conditions using a block-correlation model.

\paragraph{Per-test detection via aggregation.} Consider a buggy patch with $k = |T_{\text{f2p}}|$ fail-to-pass tests. A per-test verifier with recall $r$ (probability of correctly predicting \textsc{Fail} for a truly failing test) detects the patch as buggy if it identifies at least one failing test:
%
\begin{equation}
  P_{\text{detect}}(k) = 1 - (1 - r)^{k_{\text{eff}}}
  \label{eq:detect}
\end{equation}
%
where $k_{\text{eff}}$ is the effective number of independent tests, defined below. Under independent errors, $k_{\text{eff}} = k$ and additional tests multiplicatively reduce the miss rate $(1-r)^k$. A trajectory-level verifier, by contrast, yields a single prediction with detection probability $a_h$ independent of $k$.

\paragraph{Block-correlation model.} Per-test predictions within the same patch are not independent: a model that misunderstands the patched logic will likely err on multiple tests simultaneously. We capture this dependence using the intraclass correlation coefficient (ICC), denoted $\rho$, which measures the fraction of prediction error variance attributable to patch-level effects.

The design effect (DEFF) adjusts the nominal sample size to an effective one:
%
\begin{equation}
  \text{DEFF} = 1 + (\bar{n} - 1)\rho
  \label{eq:deff}
\end{equation}
%
where $\bar{n}$ is the mean cluster size (tests per patch). For a specific patch with $k$ F2P tests, the effective number of independent predictions is:
%
\begin{equation}
  k_{\text{eff}} = \frac{k}{1 + (k - 1)\rho}
  \label{eq:keff}
\end{equation}
%
When $\rho = 0$ (independent errors), $k_{\text{eff}} = k$ and each test provides full additional information. When $\rho = 1$ (perfectly correlated errors), $k_{\text{eff}} = 1$ regardless of $k$, and per-test prediction collapses to trajectory-level.

\paragraph{Advantage characterization.} The per-test advantage for a patch with $k$ F2P tests relative to a trajectory-level baseline with accuracy $a_h$ is:
%
\begin{equation}
  \Delta(k) = P_{\text{detect}}(k) - a_h
  \label{eq:advantage}
\end{equation}

The expected advantage over the instance population is:
%
\begin{equation}
  \mathbb{E}[\Delta] = \sum_{k=1}^{K} P(|T_{\text{f2p}}| = k) \cdot \Delta(k)
  \label{eq:expected-advantage}
\end{equation}

This framework yields three testable predictions: (1)~when $k = 1$ (single F2P test), per-test reduces to a single binary prediction and $\Delta(1) \approx 0$ assuming $r \approx a_h$; (2)~$\Delta(k)$ increases with $k$ but is dampened by $\rho > 0$, which limits the benefit of additional tests; (3)~the overall advantage $\mathbb{E}[\Delta]$ depends critically on the distribution of $k$ across instances---it concentrates in the tail of multi-F2P-test instances.

\paragraph{P2P regression detection.} Per-test verification can additionally detect regressions in pass-to-pass tests: cases where a patch breaks previously passing functionality. The value of this signal depends on the true regression rate $r_{\text{reg}}$---the fraction of P2P tests that actually fail under a buggy patch. When $r_{\text{reg}}$ is low, the expected number of true regressions per patch is small relative to the false positive rate, potentially making the P2P per-test signal net uninformative. The threshold regression rate at which per-test P2P detection becomes net positive depends on the verifier's precision on P2P tests. We evaluate this trade-off empirically in \S\ref{sec:experiments}.
